# What reaches the image: an allowlist, not a list of things to keep out.
#
# The build does `COPY . .`, and a published image is permanent, public, and
# readable layer by layer — a file deleted in a later layer is still there in
# the earlier one. So the rule is inverted: **everything is excluded**, and the
# handful of paths the build actually needs are named back in.
#
# Two reasons it is this way round rather than a list of exclusions.
#
# The first is that a denylist fails open. Anything added to the checkout later
# — a directory, a dump, a key — ships until somebody remembers to exclude it,
# and the failure is silent and public.
#
# The second is that a denylist has to *name* what it is hiding, and this file
# ships inside the image and in the repository. A list of the private things
# beside this checkout is itself the leak: it tells a reader exactly what to go
# looking for. An allowlist names only what is already public.
#
# `make docker-image` checks both ends: that nothing private matches the
# allowlist, and then that the built image really does not contain it.

*

# The app.
!src/
!static/
!drizzle/
!scripts/
!docs/

# What builds it.
!package.json
!yarn.lock
!.npmrc
!svelte.config.js
!vite.config.ts
!tsconfig.json
!drizzle.config.ts
!eslint.config.js
!.prettierrc
!.prettierignore

# What the entrypoint needs, and what a reader is owed.
!docker-entrypoint.sh
!README.md
!LICENSE
!CHANGELOG.md
!CONTRIBUTING.md
!ROADMAP.md

# Re-excluded inside the allowed directories: node_modules under any of them
# would be this machine's, compiled against this machine's Node.
**/node_modules
**/.DS_Store
